{{also|Easter}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{inh|en|ang|eastera}}, {{m|ang|eastra}}. Compare {{m|en|norther}}, {{m|en|souther}}, {{m|en|wester}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈiː.stə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈi.stəɹ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-easter.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːstə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|now|dialectal}} [[eastern|Eastern]]. {{defdate|from 8th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1824|author={{w|James Hogg}}|title=The Private Memoirs and Confessions of a Justified Sinner|page=57|publisher=Oxford|year_published=2010
|passage=In the mean while, as our apartment was a corner one, and looked both east and north, I ran to the '''easter''' casement to look after Drummond.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1828|title=The Picture of Scotland|page=187
|passage=This is properly two, if not three towns — there being an '''Easter''' Anstruther and a Wester Anstruther, both burghs, besides a large fishing village {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1885|author=Alex Johnston Warden|title=Angus Or Forfarshire: The Land and People, Descriptive and Historical|page=204
|passage=There had been a Little and a Meikle, and an '''Easter''' and a Wester Coull two centuries ago; and there had been a castle on the property {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1887|author=Walter Wood|title=The East Neuk of Fife: Its History and Antiquities|page=118
|passage=It is styled, as we have seen, Wester Rires, which implies an '''Easter''' Rires; and this last portion of it probably lay to the north-east, and included&nbsp;{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=J.I.M. Stewart|title=Mungo's Dream|publisher=House of Stratus|isbn=9780755148059|page=219
|passage=&#39;The fact remains that there is an '''Easter''' Fintry and a Wester Fintry in this part of the world. Just as there is an '''Easter''' Golford and a Wester Golford,&nbsp;...}}
# {{en-comparative of|east}}

