===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A strong easterly wind (a wind blowing from the east).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1902|author=John Burroughs|title=Locusts and Wild Honey|page=81
|passage=A northeaster in one place may be an '''easter''', a norther, or a souther in some other locality.}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To move toward the east.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1871|title=Hunt's Yachting Magazine|page=265
|passage=Off Tilbury the Alcyone&#39;s topsail-yard was carried away just forward of the slings; she set a jib-headed one; at Thames Haven the wind '''eastered'''&nbsp;...}}
#* '''1940'''(?), Thomas Allen, ''The Journals of Sir Thomas Allin, 1660-1678'':
#*: At 5 the wind '''eastered''' and came E. by N., that we went 2 knots {{...}}.

